WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.140
[... twenty bucks, you little s***!]

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.440
[don't worry about it]

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.660
[you brat] [I'm gonna get it] [f***er]

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.280
Larry: "What's going on?"

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.160
"Tell me what's going on here"

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.120
"What's going on?"

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.840
That's the question that Larry Gopnik, the ill-fated protagonist of the Coen brothers' dark comedy

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.120
'A Serious Man' keeps asking.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.560
It's a reflexive and, sort of stupid question.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:44.140
One in response to a series of unfortunate events that seem to be tearing Larry's quiet, contented,

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.920
suburban life apart without cause.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.000
First his wife demands divorce, then his student tries to blackmail him for a passing grade,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.140
then his neighbour infringes on his property, then his wife's lover dies and he's forced to pay for it,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.960
then his brother gets in trouble with 
the law and he has to pay for that too,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.100
then his chance at tenure is threatened 
by anonymous hate mail.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.400
He also gets into a car accident, witnesses the death of his lawyer,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.660
and is forced to pay for a record subscription even though...

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.400
Larry: "I didn't DO. ANYTHING."

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.820
Being a Coen brothers movie, each of these situations compound and unfold into one another

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:25.520
with surgical precision. And they are each, individually, illustrative of their own batch of themes,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:32.100
which, taken together, sort of shape out the central message of the movie like one of those optical illusions

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.940
that demonstrate closure.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:39.600
Of course, there is no real closure in 'A Serious Man', just as there's not really a shape in the illusion.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.980
That doesn't stop Larry from *seeking* it, though: he wants to understand what he's done to deserve

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.720
such misfortune, seemingly unaware of what the audience could see clear as day:

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:56.100
that it's Larry's inaction, his constant refrain of "I haven't done anything" that causes so many of his problems.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.940
Larry knows that "Actions have consequences" but inaction has consequences too.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:05.940
Instead of grappling with this fact, Larry looks for a way to interpret his suffering,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:12.560
seeking out the counsel of three rabbis. The first, a junior rabbi, lectures Larry on the importance of...

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.200
Rabbi: "Perspective. That's what it is, Larry."

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.560
The second tells a convoluted parable about a dentist who finds a message in a patient's teeth,

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.460
that never resolves itself, illustrating the inscrutability of God's plan.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:30.800
And the third rabbi, the elderly, wise rabbi Marshak, refuses to see him altogether,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.500
literalising the heavenly silence 
in the face of big questions

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.980
that prompted Larry's search 
for advice in the first place.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:43.280
A lot of the things that Larry hears are clichés, like the need to change your perspective.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.420
Rabbi: "Look at the parking lot, Larry!"

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:51.340
And yet many of them are true. Of course, cliché and parable are two devises that people use

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:56.220
to understand what's going on. And they're not the only that 'A Serious Man' has to offer.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.320
Larry's son Danny uses music and drugs, so does their next-door neighbour Mrs. Samski,

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.920
Larry's brother uses a complex probability map of the universe called 'The Mentaculous',

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.160
and Larry himself teaches the theories 
of physics and mathematics,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:16.180
namely, an equation that seems to definitively prove the underriding uncertainty of all things.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:23.280
"So I can see that it's subtle, it's clever. But at the end of the day, is it convincing?"

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.760
At its core, this film questions the persuasiveness of every interpretive device.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.120
By placing them side by side and showing 
how they fail to work together

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:38.600
and form any kind of semblance of coherence, the Coens communicate the real dread of a world

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.200
that's become unreadable.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.500
And they take this failure down to the most basic form of interpretation: the use of language.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.220
A lot of attention is payed to the language of Hebrew, an ancient language

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.720
that's lost much of its force in the modern world. Indeed, even in the 1960s,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:01.500
young Danny Gobnik was doing exactly what I did when faced with the Thora portion to learn for my bar mitswa.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:07.460
He had vinyls, he had tapes, but neither of us learned to read or understand the Hebrew.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:11.460
Instead, we memorised the sounds that, 
to us, remain gibberish.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.740
Danny: [sings in Hebrew]

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:19.780
Danny can't find his place when the time comes because those symbols are as incomprehensible

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:25.000
as 'The Mentaculous' is. And even in English, several times in the film, people are asked to repeat things,

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.540
or just hear them as nonsense.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.460
Larry: "Me sir my sir?"

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:42.180
Interpretation is how we nail down the world around us. Word by word, fable by fable, equation by equation,

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:48.780
we secure the storm of experience onto fixed points. And when these start to come undone,

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:54.220
and unforeseen circumstances are always threatening to do that, it can be very traumatic.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:05:00.140
We, like Larry, reach for anything that can orient us. Explains the appeal of religion,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:05.720
yet 'A Serious Man' documents its ever decreasing usefulness in the modern age.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.220
Of course, this criticism of interpretive methods 
ought to make us realise

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:15.020
that we're watching a form of interpretation: 
the cinematic narrative.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:20.060
'A Serious Man', a film in which every element is ambiguous, in which cause and effect,

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:25.240
the engine of any narrative, is wilfully disregarded, suggests that film and story

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:30.720
are plagued by the same problems as all the other devises, despite what Hollywood would have you believe

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.500
One of the most confounding things about the film is its opening prologue, a short fable,

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:41.820
filmed in a different aspect ratio, about a Jewish couple in the old country who stab and maybe kill

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:48.080
either a good Samaritan or a demon. The prologue's meaning and its relationship to the rest of the film

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.940
are totally nebulous. There's just enough thematic similarity to prompt interpretation.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:58.240
But once you try, the only outcome can be frustration.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.440
Moving the viewer into a head-space like that is neither pretentious, nor unwieldy.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.860
It's the effect of a subtle, experienced 
and masterful craftsmanship.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.640
The Coen brothers have been doing this for years

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.300
[I just don't understand it]

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:18.100
But 'A Serious Man' is their most explicit account of a bleak, but unflinching philosophy.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.120
Larry: "I don't understand"

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:26.140
One that remains sceptical about the things we believe, film, story and interpretation can do.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:33.480
It understands that the big, philosophical truths about human life are devastatingly simple.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:41.580
There is no God, there is no objective or cosmic purpose for us. People experience joy to varying degrees

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:48.360
but everybody suffers. Anyone can figure these things out with a small amount of observation, introspection

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:53.800
and study. And once you do, that dim feeling of disappointment will always be there.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.420
But there should also be relief, 
the kind of relief you can only get

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.740
when putting aside questions 
that are really a waste of time.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:06.700
Because only when the reflexive and sort of stupid questions are dropped,

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.500
can the better questions be asked.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:12.340
Better questions like whether or not the mind 
